  "crow_answers_text": {
    "t3-debug": "# Root Cause\n\n## The Bug: `Get-Content -Raw` and the BOM\n\nThe script reads the file with **`Get-Content -Raw`**, which on Windows PowerShell (5.1) **preserves the the UTF- BOM** as part of the returned string. When that string is then serialized by `ConvertTo-Json`, the BOM character (`U+FEFF`) becomes a raw codepoint inside the JSON value. On the wire, this encodes to the three-byte sequence `EF BB BF` — but crucially, **the JSON spec forbids a bare BOM inside a string value**. A strict JSON parser (like the one behind the llama.cpp server) sees the byte after the opening quote and trips its ill-formed-UTF8 check at exactly the wrong column.\n\nIn short:\n\n```\nprompt.txt  →  Get-Content -Raw  →  \"$prompt\" contains U+FEFF at position 0\n                                    ↓\n                    ConvertTo-Json serializes it into the body\n                                    ↓\n              Server's JSON parser rejects the embedded BOM\n```\n\n---\n\n## Why Both Checks Come Back Clean\n\n### Check 1: \"pure ASCII / valid UTF-8 / no bytes above 127\"\n\nThis was almost certainly done by inspecting the **file bytes on disk** (e.g., `[System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes('prompt.txt')`). The BOM lives in the **PowerShell string object in memory**, not in the file itself. If you hex-dump the `prompt.txt`, you see only clean ASCII — the BOM is an artifact of how `-Raw` materializes the .NET `String`. So the file-level check correctly reports \"clean,\" yet the in-memory string is already corrupted.\n\n### Check 2: `$prompt.GetType()` returns `System.String`; `.Length` = 15831\n\nBoth are true and both are expected. A BOM-prefixed string is still a perfectly well-formed `System.String` — the BOM is just another code point. And `.Length` counts characters, so it includes the BOM silently. Neither property distinguishes a BOM-led string from a clean one. You'd need to inspect the first char explicitly:\n\n```powershell\n[int][char]$prompt[0]   # 65279 (= 0xFEFF) if BOM present; otherwise normal text\n```\n\nThe diagnostics never looked at the *content* of the first character, only at type and length, so they missed the smoking gun.\n\n---\n\n## Smallest Correct Fix\n\nReplace the read cmdlet so the BOM is stripped before serialization:\n\n```powershell\n$prompt = [IO.File]::ReadAllText('prompt.txt', [Text.Encoding]::UTF8)\n# or equivalently, on PS 5.1:\n# $prompt = (Get-Content 'prompt.txt' -Encoding utf8) -join \"`n\"\n```\n\n`File.ReadAllText(path, Encoding.UTF8)` **detects and strips** the BOM per the .NET contract, yielding a clean string whose first code point is the real content. No other line changes.\n\nIf you must stay within pure PowerShell cmdlets, the equivalent minimal change is:\n\n```powershell\n$prompt = (Get-Content 'prompt.txt' -Encoding Byte | ForEach-Object { ... })\n```\n\n…but the single-line fix above is cleaner and idiomatic.\n\n> **Alternative:** keep `Get-Content -Raw` and strip manually:\n> ```powershell\n> $prompt = $prompt.TrimStart([char]0xFEFF)\n> ```\n> This also works and is arguably the smallest possible diff.\n\n---\n\n## Two Other Cmdlets That Silently Inject the Same Corruption\n\nAny cmdlet that round-trips through a **.NET `String`** without explicit encoding control will carry the BOM forward into downstream consumers:\n\n| Cmdlet | Why it bites |\n|---|---|\n| **`Set-Content -Value $someString`** (default encoding) | Writes the BOM back out, producing double-BOM'd files that break subsequent parsers. |\n| **`Out-File`** / **`Add-Content`** (no `-Encoding` specified) | Inherits the same default behavior; the resulting file now has a leading `EF BB BF` even when the source had none originally. |\n\nA third example worth noting: **`Invoke-RestMethod` / `Invoke-WebRequest`** when given a `-Body` that came from any of the above — the HTTP layer sends the corrupt bytes verbatim, exactly as seen here."
  },
